From 6bd3fe743565a26b91a095694400116375498d9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wenbo Ji <36562829+fusheng-ji@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:53:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(h3): build the backward segment tiles once per row index

The stable sort and tile metadata behind the deterministic gate, shift and
scale gradients depend only on the row index, but every norm, gated residual
and gather rebuilt them in backward (about ten small torch ops, two of which
synchronise with the host). A block backward does that four times for one
index.

Keep the last eight results keyed by the source tensors' storage, shape and
autograd version, and have the five callers (rmsnorm, gate_residual,
final_adaln_out, adaln_row_gather, adaln_modulation) go through it. An
in-place edit of the index bumps its version and builds again; the entry keeps
its sources alive so a freed address cannot alias another index; a rejected
repeat count is never cached. The metadata is read-only in every kernel and the
kernels are unchanged, so the gradients are bitwise what they were.

Tests: build-once, no aliasing of an equal index in other storage, in-place
invalidation, bounded size, a rejected repeat is rejected on every call, and a
CUDA check that cached and uncached gate/norm gradients are bitwise equal.

Signed-off-by: Wenbo Ji <36562829+fusheng-ji@users.noreply.github.com>
